      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Chronic pain in the perineum is a difficult neurosurgical problem. This article evaluates the effectiveness of sacrococcygeal rhizotomy in 28 patients who had cancer-related pain or coccydynia, underwent rhizotomy, and were followed for an average of 3 years. Good pain relief was obtained in 53% (10 of 19 patients) with malignant pain, as opposed to 22% (2 of 9 patients) with nonmalignant pain. Sacral rhizotomy is a reasonable treatment for cancer-related perineal pain, but it is ineffective for coccydynia and other benign perineal pain problems."},
